{"id":"services-networking-windows-networking","corpus":"k8s","title":"Networking on Windows","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/windows-networking/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Networking on Windows"},{"kind":"p","text":"Kubernetes supports running nodes on either Linux or Windows. You can mix both kinds of node within a single cluster. This page provides an overview to networking specific to the Windows operating system."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Container networking on Windows"},{"kind":"p","text":"Networking for Windows containers is exposed through CNI plugins. Windows containers function similarly to virtual machines in regards to networking. Each container has a virtual network adapter (vNIC) which is connected to a Hyper-V virtual switch (vSwitch). The Host Networking Service (HNS) and the Host Compute Service (HCS) work together to create containers and attach container vNICs to networks. HCS is responsible for the management of containers whereas HNS is responsible for the management of networking resources such as:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Virtual networks (including creation of vSwitches)"},{"kind":"li","text":"Endpoints / vNICs"},{"kind":"li","text":"Namespaces"},{"kind":"li","text":"Policies including packet encapsulations, load-balancing rules, ACLs, and NAT rules."},{"kind":"p","text":"The Windows HNS and vSwitch implement namespacing and can create virtual NICs as needed for a pod or container. However, many configurations such as DNS, routes, and metrics are stored in the Windows registry database rather than as files inside /etc, which is how Linux stores those configurations. The Windows registry for the container is separate from that of the host, so concepts like mapping /etc/resolv.conf from the host into a container don't have the same effect they would on Linux. These must be configured using Windows APIs run in the context of that container. Therefore CNI implementations need to call the HNS instead of relying on file mappings to pass network details into the pod or container."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Network modes"},{"kind":"p","text":"Windows supports five different networking drivers/modes: L2bridge, L2tunnel, Overlay (Beta), Transparent, and NAT. In a heterogeneous cluster with Windows and Linux worker nodes, you need to select a networking solution that is compatible on both Windows and Linux. The following table lists the out-of-tree plugins are supported on Windows, with recommendations on when to use each CNI:"},{"kind":"p","text":"As outlined above, the Flannel CNI plugin is also supported on Windows via the VXLAN network backend (Beta support ; delegates to win-overlay) and host-gateway network backend (stable support; delegates to win-bridge)."},{"kind":"p","text":"This plugin supports delegating to one of the reference CNI plugins (win-overlay, win-bridge), to work in conjunction with Flannel daemon on Windows (Flanneld) for automatic node subnet lease assignment and HNS network creation. This plugin reads in its own configuration file (cni.conf), and aggregates it with the environment variables from the FlannelD generated subnet.env file. It then delegates to one of the reference CNI plugins for network plumbing, and sends the correct configuration containing the node-assigned subnet to the IPAM plugin (for example: host-local)."},{"kind":"p","text":"For Node, Pod, and Service objects, the following network flows are supported for TCP/UDP traffic:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Pod → Pod (IP)"},{"kind":"li","text":"Pod → Pod (Name)"},{"kind":"li","text":"Pod → Service (Cluster IP)"},{"kind":"li","text":"Pod → Service (PQDN, but only if there are no \".\")"},{"kind":"li","text":"Pod → Service (FQDN)"},{"kind":"li","text":"Pod → external (IP)"},{"kind":"li","text":"Pod → external (DNS)"},{"kind":"li","text":"Node → Pod"},{"kind":"li","text":"Pod → Node"},{"kind":"h2","text":"IP address management (IPAM)"},{"kind":"p","text":"The following IPAM options are supported on Windows:"},{"kind":"li","text":"host-local"},{"kind":"li","text":"azure-vnet-ipam (for azure-cni only)"},{"kind":"li","text":"Windows Server IPAM (fallback option if no IPAM is set)"},{"kind":"h2","text":"Direct Server Return (DSR)"},{"kind":"p","text":"More information about this feature"},{"kind":"p","text":"This is a stable feature in Kubernetes, and has been since version v1.34. It was first available in the v1.14 release. You can no longer disable or opt out of this feature or behavior (it is locked); if you explicitly set a value for the associated feature gate WinDSR, Kubernetes ignores it but does not report any error."},{"kind":"p","text":"Load balancing mode where the IP address fixups and the LBNAT occurs at the container vSwitch port directly; service traffic arrives with the source IP set as the originating pod IP. This provides performance optimizations by allowing the return traffic routed through load balancers to bypass the load balancer and respond directly to the client; reducing load on the load balancer and also reducing overall latency. For more information, read Direct Server Return (DSR) in a nutshell."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Load balancing and Services"},{"kind":"p","text":"A Kubernetes Service is an abstraction that defines a logical set of Pods and a means to access them over a network. In a cluster that includes Windows nodes, you can use the following types of Service:"}],"lemmas":{"networking":["networking","network"],"networkings":["networkings","networking"],"windows":["window","windows"],"kubernetes":["kubernetes","kubernete"],"supports":["support","supports"],"supporting":["supporting","support"],"running":["running","run"],"runnings":["running","runnings"],"runs":["run","runs"],"ran":["ran","run"],"nodes":["node","nod","nodes"],"nods":["nod","nods"],"nodded":["nodded","nod"],"nodding":["noddin